Yeah. Think about what it meant to be a good friend 30 years ago. You could go a week without hearing from somebody and assume they were busy. Now they can see that you read their message three days ago. The capacity to care is familiar. What counts as evidence that you care has changed. A read receipt can turn silence into something that feels deliberate.

And those changes can go deeper than expression. If you spend years being reachable, you may start to feel that leaving someone unanswered is rude, even when nobody is checking. A feature became an expectation. Then the expectation became part of your own judgment.

That loops back to Matthew Perry. Friends was the part of his life that millions of people could revisit together. Shared attention gave that part of him a kind of permanence. A private moment could have mattered just as much to him and left almost no trace in the public memory.

There’s a Pantheon experiment in that. Give the same character the same impulse in 1986 and 2026: “I want my friend to know I’m still here.” In one scene he leaves a message on an answering machine. In the other he sends a text, sees it was read, and spends the afternoon wondering whether he said the wrong thing. He started with the same impulse. By dinner, he’s lived a different emotional day. Give him forty years of those days and you can see how a change in the interface becomes a change in the person.